## Context

The author asked to ticket the bounded SDT worker pool discussed after the
padme measurements. The experiment and its limitations are recorded in
`verification/SDT-PARALLELISM-0674.md`, with raw evidence under
`bench/results/0674-sdt-padme/`. The demonstrated parallelism is between
independent Node processes using native ONNX, not between Zotero Web Workers.
Do not promise its speedup for the Zotero patch.

The target is Zotero desktop's
`chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/pdfWorker/manager.js`, with the SDT service in
`chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/sdt.js` and whichever bulk producer feeds it.
The installed source and the Zotero main file read on 2026-09-05 both use a
singleton worker and await each queue task before starting the next. SDT
requests enter that queue. Installed source hashes and anchors are in the
measurement artifact's `queue-provenance.json`.

Source: https://github.com/zotero/zotero/blob/main/chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/pdfWorker/manager.js

The local zoteus fork's `src/features/search/build.ts` consumes a full-text
source; its fetch concurrency is not the Zotero extraction worker pool. Read
`SYNC.md`'s Zotero core/extraction rows and refresh the Zotero source and draft
semantic-search work before implementation so a newly shipped pool or changed
producer is not duplicated.

## Proposed experiment

Prototype an SDT-only pool of independent Web Workers, retaining the existing
extractor, models and pack format. Keep the pool size configurable for the
experiment. Compare a serial control, a pair of workers, and a four-worker arm
on padme. The pair was suggested as a conservative initial default, with the
serial path as fallback; these are experiment candidates, not a ratified
shipping default or a requirement to occupy every CPU core. Select a proposed
shipping setting from actual Zotero throughput, peak memory and reader
responsiveness, then use the repository's decision process if that choice
changes the system contract. SPEC.md remains the owner of design limits.

## Work

- Pin the Zotero checkout and document-worker revision. Inspect the bulk caller:
  an await-per-document producer must feed a bounded window of requests or the
  new pool will remain idle. Keep queued requests lightweight and defer loading
  PDF buffers until a worker slot is available.
- Introduce a dispatcher and independent worker state: request IDs, pending
  promises, progress, asset replies and errors must route back to the correct
  originating worker. Warm workers can be reused, with an explicit idle and
  shutdown lifecycle.
- Preserve interactive priority and the existing same-item in-flight
  deduplication. Keep PDF mutations on their serialized path and coordinate
  same-file extraction with rotation, page deletion and annotation writes;
  retaining a separate mutation queue alone does not prevent those races.
- Integrate the caller's pause/stop lifecycle. Define what happens to active
  jobs, prevent new background starts while paused, and preserve progress and
  cache validation against source changes. Reject pending promises on worker
  failure and ensure another document can still complete afterward.
- Measure in an isolated Zotero profile using the actual Web Worker/WASM path.
  Include mixed document lengths and layouts, concurrent reader requests, and
  genuine uncached extraction. Distinguish warm worker reuse from cold startup;
  include pack writing, process-wide peak memory and observed CPU utilization.
  Do not substitute native ONNX for WASM when pricing this patch.
- Record raw evidence and a report under the existing repository conventions.
  Compare pack contents after normalizing timestamps and check page coverage,
  block structure, outline, anchors and extraction fallback/error reporting.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Reviewable isolated Zotero patch dispatches SDT work to independent
      workers within the configured bound; tests prove overlap and the bound.
- [ ] Priority, same-item deduplication, same-file mutation coordination,
      progress routing, pause/stop and crash recovery have meaningful tests.
- [ ] Real Zotero measurements compare the proposed pool settings with serial
      extraction, including throughput, peak memory and reader responsiveness.
- [ ] Output preservation and failure behavior are verified; limitations and
      the proposed default/fallback are justified by the resulting evidence.
- [ ] Zotero's relevant tests and this repository's `make check` pass; the patch
      and evidence are available for review.

## Scope

No Rust rewrite, parser replacement, GPU backend or automatic use of all cores
is needed for this experiment. Ticket 0674 is completed evidence, not an open
blocker. Ticket 0676 investigates a specific document's phase/memory behavior
and can inform corpus selection without blocking a scheduling prototype.
This is a local work ticket; upstream publication is a separate authorized
step governed by `GOVERNANCE.md`.
